MSVC compatibility for LibBF

Upstream LibBF targets GCC/Clang. setup-libbf.sh applies this patch to the
pristine release tarball right after unpacking, so what STP builds is
upstream plus exactly this delta; a failed hunk on a version bump means
the patch needs rebasing. Each change site carries an [STP] comment.

cutils.h gains a cl.exe branch (clang-cl keeps the upstream one) supplying
what MSVC lacks: likely/unlikely/force_inline/no_inline/__maybe_unused as
MSVC equivalents or no-ops, clz32/clz64/ctz32/ctz64 over _BitScanReverse*/
_BitScanForward* (cf. https://stackoverflow.com/a/68360512), #pragma pack
for the packed structs, and a CUTILS_FORMAT_PRINTF macro replacing the
bare printf-format attribute (cutils.c uses it on the definition too).
libbf.h keeps every Windows compiler on the 32-bit limb path: clang-cl
defines __SIZEOF_INT128__ but has no 128-bit division runtime when
targeting MSVC (https://github.com/bellard/quickjs/issues/14).

--- a/libbf.h
+++ b/libbf.h
@@ -27,7 +27,13 @@
 #include <stddef.h>
 #include <stdint.h>
 
-#if defined(__SIZEOF_INT128__) && (INTPTR_MAX >= INT64_MAX)
+/* [STP] The && !defined(_WIN32) keeps every Windows compiler on the
+   32-bit limb path: clang-cl defines __SIZEOF_INT128__ but has no 128-bit
+   division runtime (__udivti3) when targeting MSVC, see
+   https://github.com/bellard/quickjs/issues/14. cl.exe never defines it
+   and lands there anyway. One of the local modifications listed in
+   scripts/deps/patches/. */
+#if defined(__SIZEOF_INT128__) && (INTPTR_MAX >= INT64_MAX) && !defined(_WIN32)
 #define LIMB_LOG2_BITS 6
 #else
 #define LIMB_LOG2_BITS 5
